<key>approval</key><dict>
<key>rejectedCandidate</key><string>Modulune; Rove; Quell; Mote; Rill; BitDeck; Bit Deck; Bit-Deck</string>
<key>rejectionReason</key><string>Human feedback on 2026-08-27 rejected Modulune as too long and multi-syllabic, then rejected Rove and its generic short-name slate as not evocative of 1990s/2000s music-player culture. A subsequent exact-spelling screen rejected BitDeck and its spaced and hyphenated forms: current indexed sources identify BitDeck, LLC as a software business using bitdeck.com, a BitDeck mobile-app developer/listing, a federal BITDECK filing for downloadable mobile applications, and an unrelated bitdeck software package. This is a screening finding, not a legal opinion.</string>
<key>state</key><string>rescreen-required</string>
</dict>
<key>attorneyReviewRequired</key><true/>
<key>candidateBrief</key><dict>
<key>excludedTermsAndImagery</key><array><string>Sirius</string><string>XM</string><string>SiriusXM</string><string>official-affiliation wording</string><string>provider logos or dog imagery</string><string>channel artwork</string><string>Apple hardware</string><string>standard-control facsimiles</string><string>other players' trade dress</string></array>
<key>goods</key><string>Native macOS music and radio player for subscribers</string>
<key>style</key><string>Late-1990s/early-2000s desktop music-player culture: burned CDs, MP3 bitrates, bitmap skins, spectrum scopes, equalizers, and chunky desktop hardware. Prefer one or two easy syllables; a short two-word compound is acceptable when it names a real era-specific idea rather than a forced portmanteau.</string>
</dict>
<key>decisionScope</key><string>Product identity proposal only; this record does not authorize SiriusXM service access, use of provider marks, or a public launch.</string>
<key>legalClearanceClaim</key><false/>
